    "q-300982": {
      "dom_id": "step-q300982",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{7}{4{(x-2)}^{3}}\\,=$\n {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{7}{4{(x-2)}^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x-2)}^{3}}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${(x-2)}^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x-2)}^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot 0=0\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
